I use rpm -bb.  That will "install" the source, compile it and build
binary packages/RPMs.  rpm -bb will not automagically install the RPMs
after they are built.  You still have to install the RPMs as usual as root.

Here's what I did last night to make a local ev56 version of mozilla 1.1.
I put my edited mozilla-1.1.spec file in /usr/src/redhat/SPECS and the
source and patches from the source RPM in /usr/src/redhat/SOURCES.
Then in SPECS I did a "rpm -bb mozilla-1.1.spec".  RPM untarred the sources
into a subdirectory in /usr/src/redhat/BUILD, applied the patches specified
in the spec file, ran configure and then make.  In a few hours it was done
and I had binary RPMs in /usr/src/redhat/RPMS/alpha.  Also, it does not
clean up the BUILD directory after it is done.  You have to do that 
yourself.  But then again, I could be missing something in reading the
confusing doc...

Alan

