I've been trying to get a sound card to work in my alpha for the
past week to no avail. If anybody can help me I'll be most grateful.

The problem is that whenever I go to play any sound, the system
completely freezes so that I have to reboot.

My system is a 533 MHZ PC164LX with AlphaBios 5.66 and RH 7.1. This
sound card is PCI, and I have one other PCI card (my Matrox video
card) as well as two older ISA cards (modem and parallel port card).

Here's what I've gone through so far:

First I bought a Soundblaster 16PCI card. When I put it in, kudzu
recognized it. When I then ran sndconfig, it played the sample,
but it then froze the system and kept repeating the sample ad nauseum.
When I researched this, I found other people with the same problem
so I tried some suggestions:

1. Rebuilding kernel with es1371 driver built in
2. Trying patch someone posted for es1371 driver.

Eventually I gave up because it wasn't working and there were so many
posts about trouble with that card. Instead I got a SB 512 card, with
the EMU10K1 chipset. Here I get basically the same behavior, except
usually it doesn't even play the sample or any music.

With this card, I've just been running the stock 7.1 kernel. At boot
time I get these PCI messages:
got res[8000:801f] for resource 0 of SB Live! EMU10000
got res[8030:8037] for resource 0 of SB Live!

I also get lines that say "PCI enable device".

I have an alias line in modules.conf
alias sound-slot-0 emu10k1, and in dmesg the driver loads fine,
although there is an error "Can't locate module sound-services-0".

After booting up, if I check /dev/sndstat, it tells me "no such device",
and if I try to reference /dev/audio or /dev/dsp, or to
play music using xmms or mpg123 the system freezes. Great.

I can get sound through playing the CDROM, though.

Here's what I've tried so far with this 512 card:

1. Tried putting the 512 card in each of the other PCI cards, including
switching it with the video card.
2. Tried removing older ISA cards
3. Tried playing around with modules.conf
4. Tried upgrading to newest version of EMU10K1 driver from sourceforge

Next I might try:

1. building emu10k1 into the kernel
2. trying other drivers (alsa, oss $$)
3. trying with different kernel

Is there something basic I've missed with IRQs or io addresses?
I don't know a whole lot about that sort of thing, so if someone
could point me in the right direction, that'd be great.

Another thought I had is that a couple months ago there was a big
thunderstorm that knocked out my modem, and also took with it
my onboard parallel port (power for the alpha was on a surge protector,
butthe modem line wasn't). Maybe that futzed up my motherboard? Are
there any diagnostic tools I can try?

Any help anybody could give I'd be really happy. I'm beating my
head against the wall here. :-)

Allen




