I haven't seen much traffic on the list for while.  Here is a question
for you guys.

I am trying to divide two 8 bit images (A*256/B) as fast as possible.
I built a lookup table of all possible combinations of A & B (64KB)
and do the lookup on the images.  On a 2.0GHz P4, it takes about
28msec on a 1280x1024x24 images.  On the 1.0GHz ev68 (at Compaq
testdrive), it takes about 57msec, which is very disappointing.  I was
expecting the Alpha to be much faster due to its larger L1 cache and
faster memory IO.  The relevant code is below.  I tried both gcc -O6
and ccc -O3.

pTab is the pre-constructed 256x256 lookup table, with numerator as
the row index and denominator as the column index.

typedef unsigned char pixel8;

  for (l=0; l<100; ++l)
  {
	pixel8* pOut = output_buf;
	const pixel8* pN = input_buf;
	const pixel8* pD = white_buf;
	int r;

	for (r=0; r< HEIGHT; ++r)
	  {
		int c;
		for (c=0; c< WIDTH*3; ++c)
		  {
			int i = ((*pN)<<8) + (*pD);
			*pOut = pTab[i];
			++pN;
			++pD;
			++pOut;
		  }
	  }
  }

Any idea?

Regards,

