On Tuesday, 10 Sep, Rui-Tao Dong wrote:
> I am trying to divide two 8 bit images (A*256/B) as fast as possible.
> I built a lookup table of all possible combinations of A & B (64KB)
> and do the lookup on the images.

64K is a lot for a lookup table and will definitely cause on-chip cache
thrashing.  It is often faster to compute than to look up.  I don't know
how fast multiplication is on EV68, but there is a slim chance that
performance can be improved by replacing the division table with a table
of inverted values of B (scaled to be integer - say, 2^24/B) and
multiplying A by pTab[B] in the loop.  The table will shrink to 1 KB,
hopefully letting enough space in the cache for the other buffers.

> I tried both gcc -O6 and ccc -O3.

Try ccc -fast and gcc -mcpu=ev67 (or -mcpu=ev6 if using gcc 2) -O6.  This
will utilise more efficient byte loads available in EV6.

On Tuesday, 10 Sep, Chris Eleveld wrote:
> To avoid unaligned accesses flatten the array out to 64 bit longs.
> Either using bit shifting or as a larger array.  I believe single byte
> accesses are the worst on alpha but I could be wrong.

Single byte reads were not too bad even in the original Alpha architecture -
hardly worse than bit shifting or byte extraction written in a high
level language.  They were indeed slower than 32 and 64 bit loads.  Byte
writes were pretty expensive though.  EV6 has instructions for single byte
load/store.

> Also there should
> be some architecture switches to take advantage oft EV68 instructions
> for dealing with bytes. I think.

Yes, you are right.

Nikita




