Hi,

On Tue, 17 Sep 2002 23:57:18 -0700 (PDT)
chu@tes-mail.jpl.nasa.gov (Eugene Chu) wrote:

> Are these any particular matrix transpose routines?  That was one of
> the holy grails in images processing:  an efficient corner-turn
> implementation.  The other was a fast random indexed addressing of
> arrays.  They were always very good at incurring TLB misses.

The matrix transpose benchmark routine is written with usual C.
The super page kernel is transparent to applications and
you don't have to rewrite your program at all.
If you have alligned your memory request to 4MB in length and
turning on the vm_align option of the kernel, you will have
a little more performance gain. But basically, Alpha and Sparc64
does not need such a spell.

> 
> Also, I'm guessing there is no such feature on a Pentium or Athlon
> processor?
> 

I tested with Pentium-4 and a contributor tests on Athlon. Both had a 
good improvements. But for Pentium-3, it does not improved the performance much.

PS: Thank you George, I hope we can meet at Charlottesville.

Naohiko




