> > From: Dan Kirkpatrick <dkirk@physics.syr.edu>
> > Date: Tue, 17 Sep 2002 12:28:55 -0400
> >

> > I have a DEC Alpha machine that use to run DEC unix/tru64... now it's 
> > running RedHat Linux 7.0 for alpha and the disk is dying... so I put in a 
> > new hard drive and copied everything over to the new disk with
> > dump/restore.
> > Now on boot, it says bootstrap failure... block 0 of dka0.0.0.1001.0 is
> > not a valid boot block.
> >
> > What's the magic way to create the boot block as well?
> > It's running Linux now so I don't have 'disklabel' and the original disk 
> > lost it's boot block in trying to copy it over to the new disk :(
> 
> The Alpha Installation Guide, which I believe was included in the
> RH6.1 and RH7.0 boxed product, and which should be available on
> Redhat's web site, describes how write aboot to a disk.  There
> should also be an aboot how-to document around that describes
> it.  I can't find it in my hardcopy at the moment, but I seem to
> recall the command is 'swriteboot'.  There may be man pages.

Yes, even the IG with RH 7.23 for Alpha includes such... :-)

And there is a man page under 7.2 as well, for "swriteboot".

Why not use CD #1 of RH 7.2 for Alpha as a "rescue" disk? That's one
of the aboot.conf options, and will allow such rescue work as is needed.

Just remember, there has to be sufficient space (~2MB?) in front of the
first partition to allow bootstrap install (another IG section :-).

Good luck.

