> 
> I have a Smart UPS 1400 here and it just 'works' in linux / windows on 
> both intel and AXP. I use the actual APC daemon in x86 linux, and run 
> upsd on the AXP one as APC doesn't want to come out with an AXP version of 
> its software =/.

NUT ( http://www.exploits.org/nut/ ) has a pretty good support for
many APC models.  I strongly suspect that in general better then
what APC provides. :-)  There are even nut rpms from Red Hat which you
can recompile on your Alpha but they have messed up a bit permissions
and some other details in configuration files (nothing really serious
but it should be fixed) and there are quite a bit behind in versions.

A list of supported hardware can be found at
http://www.exploits.org/nut/library/compat.html
On the same site there is also a detailed information about APC
cables with cable model numbers (see FAQ), colours  and pictures.

In general when installing UPS support software keep in mind that
utilities required in an orderly shutdown _cannot_ reside on
partitions which can be unmounted before or while these things
are still needed. :-) /sbin is usually a safe place while places
like /usr/local/{sbin,bin} or /usr/{sbin,bin} may or may not be
depending on your disk layout.

   Michal




