Hello everybody,

I was trying to switch my PC164LX to use SRM instead of AlphaBIOS. I
installed the SRM 5.8 firmware, and I created a boot floppy according
to the instructions in the SRM howto. However, when I try to boot
using the command

boot dva0 -file vmlinux.gz -flags "root=/dev/hdc2"

it hangs after printing out "jumping to bootstrap code".

I tried the following:
- downloading the latest aboot source and building that
- using different kernel images
- using different "root=" parameters
- I can run the installer fine from my Red Hat 7.1 install
CDROM, or from some older 6.0 floppies I had around.
- tried copying the "linuxboot" file from the install media that
does work
- I noticed someone else had the same problem with an SX on this list,
but there were no responses to that post.

I had these questions:

1. My hard drives don't have BSD disklabels. Does this just
prevent me from booting off of them? Or does it mean I can't
even boot off of a floppy while setting the root to my hard drive?

2. Could this have to do with what kernel image I'm using? I built
the kernel with just some minor modifications from the stock
configuration (changing alpha type to PC164, setting boot by SRM to yes,
and turning off some of the sound settings). However I did have problems
with compiling the modules. I got compile errors having to do with defines
in ksyms.ver and
alpha_syms.ver. I just gave up on the modules since I figured what was
already installed was basically the same anyway.

If anybody has any ideas, that would be great!

Allen Ziegenfus




