Michal Jaegermann wrote:
> 
> On Tue, Sep 24, 2002 at 10:59:01AM -0400, alvin wrote:
> > Chris Szilagyi wrote:
> > >
> > > The solution to this problem is to
> > > add "-fno-strength-reduce" to CFLAGS in arch/alpha/Makefile and recompile
> > > the entire kernel.
> 
> But this affects files in a subdirectory arch/alpha or these flags
> are inherited by the entire kernel?
> 
> >
> > I actaully ended up finding the offending code in the md driver I
> > believe that this is a bug in the gcc optimizer but I had no luck
> > creating a small test case and the gcc boys don't like being sent a full
> > kernel as an example of the bug.
> >
> > the offending code was:
> >
> >                 new_disk--;
> >                 if (new_disk == disk) {
> 
> And this code is drivers/md/raid1.c so adding "-fno-strength-reduce"
> only in this subdirectory should provide a workaround as well.  Right?

The downside here is that if there are any "xxx--; if ( xxx == yyy)"
statments in the code these will also misbehave. I have at times seen
some strange crashes with the 2.4.18 kernels I built but I was not sure
if the reason was because of this bug showing its head. I like the
-fno-strength-reduce if it makes the problem go away throughout the
whole kernel.

I have to wonder though if the boys at HP/Compaq/Dec actally tested the
kernel with a raid1 drive set. With my raid1 boot patches my systems did
not even get as far as single user mode without crashing because of this
bug. I get the impression that the bug would also make a regular raid1
non root use would also fail quickly.

 
> You mean that if you are recompiling only 'raid1.c' code, not
> mentioning 'raid1_read_balance ()' function then this bug is not showing
> up?  AFAIK an optimizer in gcc is not crossing file boundaries.

I found and replaced a number of cases of "xxx--; if ( xxx == yyy)" that
were in raid1.c and that made the problem go away.

> > and breaks on the test of new_disk in the if statement. by changing the
> > code to
> >
> >                 new_disk--;
> >                 md_bugcheck(disk);
> >                 if (new_disk == disk) {
> >
> > where md_bugcheck does nothing but return caused the optimizer to handle
> > the case differently.
> 
> This is not that surprising. 'md_bugcheck' may have side effects

Just to be on the safe side I created md_bugcheck another file and
declared it as external. I knew that the gcc optimizer could optimize
out the null procedure if it was in the same file. 



