On Wed, Sep 25, 2002 at 11:01:08PM -0400, Allen Ziegenfus wrote:
> 
> One question -- is there a way I can just mount the root filesystem
> readonly so that when the soundcard crashes my computer,

Not really.  The main trouble is that with current setups there
is a number entries in /dev which change permissions and/or
ownership when you are logging on a console so you need read/write
access there.  There are ways around but none I know of is truly
satisfactory.  Probably the best one is to use only remote logins;
this would also cut to a great extent your access to a sound card :-)
Check for detaile files under /etc/security/, 'man pam_console'
are related docs.

> I don't
> have to go through the lengthy task of checking the filesystem
> when I reboot?

Why then you will not use some journaling filesystem - like ext3?
The other excellent method is to keep a filesystem mounted on / small.
There are no real problems to mount read-only anything under /usr.

> I noticed in the rc files that
> it explicitly mounts the root file system as read write there. How
> can I override that?

Nothing really prevents you from remounting file systems ro in rc.local.
You also have /etc/fstab to specify "normal" options used by 'mount'.

> SRM seems pretty nice, but I do notice that my clock is wildly off
> most of the time.

Hm.  NVRAM battery is going?

  Michal




